WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
Hey, everyone. Welcome to Models and Agents Episode 26 for April 5th, 2026.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.180
Let's dive into what happened in the AI world today.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:10.500
And trust me, it's been one of those genuinely exciting days

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.140
where you can feel the pace of progress accelerating.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.840
The top story of this episode is one of those moments that makes you sit back and smile

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.160
because it directly attacks one of the biggest complaints in the agent space.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:25.340
Kevin Goo open-sourced auto agent, a system that builds a meta agent

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:29.480
capable of autonomously upgrading its own harness until it reaches number one

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.300
on whatever target task you give it.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.160
The harness here refers to everything outside the raw model,

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.280
the system prompts, the tool definitions, the output schemas,

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.100
the evaluation logic, the few shot examples,

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:47.140
basically all the brittle scaffolding that usually makes agents frustrating to deploy in production.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.000
What's particularly elegant is that auto agent uses the same base model,

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.680
in this case, Claude, for both execution and evaluation.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.040
This shared architecture gives it superior failure analysis and iteration quality

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.820
compared to human oversight or using a different judge model.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:05.920
The meta agent doesn't just guess what's wrong,

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:10.060
it understands the exact quirks, tokenization behavior,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.900
and instruction following tendencies of the executor because they're the same model family.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:19.340
In under 24 hours, this system topped terminal bench for coding tasks

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.020
and completely dominated spreadsheet-based financial modeling benchmarks.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:25.000
That's not a small achievement.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.160
This result directly challenges the common complaint that agents suck.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.660
Auto agent suggests that the real limiter has rarely been the underlying model itself.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.540
It's almost always been the quality of the harness.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.140
Once you remove the human bottleneck from the optimization loop,

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.400
the performance jump can be dramatic.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.920
For practitioners, this is huge.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:49.580
You can now point this system at almost any domain-specific task,

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.720
legal contract analysis, financial forecasting,

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.880
DevOps automation, scientific literature review,

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.200
and let itself optimize into a specialized expert.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:02.160
The full implementation is already available on GitHub

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.400
and the barrier to experimentation is remarkably low.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:06.280
My take?

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:11.480
This is one of the most important practical advances in agent development we've seen in the last year.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:15.300
It shifts the mindset from how do I prompt this model better

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:20.160
to how do I build a system that discovers the best possible harness for my use case?

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:24.180
The biggest limiter for agent performance isn't intelligence.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.140
It's configuration quality.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:30.560
Auto agent turns that configuration problem into an optimization problem

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:31.780
the model can solve itself.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:36.700
Let's stay on this theme for a moment because the engineering insight here is deeper than it first appears.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:41.280
Everyone talks about self-improving agents as if the model simply gets smarter

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.260
through some magical feedback loop.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.520
In practice, auto agents real breakthrough is that it treats the harness itself

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:52.380
as the primary optimization target rather than trying to improve model weights.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.540
The core insight is that most agent failures are actually systematic

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:59.120
and reproducible once you have a reliable critic.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.460
By using the same model family for both acting and judging,

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:06.960
the critic shares the exact same strengths, weaknesses, tokenization quirks,

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:09.720
and instruction following patterns as the executor.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:13.820
This creates much tighter, more coherent feedback than a human reviewer

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.800
or a different model family can provide.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:19.080
The meta agent then performs a kind of program synthesis

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:23.020
over the entire configuration space, mutating system prompts,

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:25.920
adjusting JSON schemas, rewriting tool descriptions,

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.260
changing error recovery strategies,

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:32.160
and continuously measuring success on a held out validation set.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.140
Of course, this comes with overhead.

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:38.560
Each iteration requires multiple full test runs which can get expensive,

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:42.560
but it completely removes the human bottleneck that traditionally made

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:47.580
high-quality domain specific agent tuning prohibitively expensive and slow.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:52.820
The quality gains are most pronounced on long horizon or highly structured tasks

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:57.500
where brittle tool calling, prompt drift, or subtle schema mismatches

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.180
usually kill performance after a few steps.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:03.020
That said, there's an important gotcha.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:07.060
The meta optimization process itself needs careful bounding.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:09.700
Without limits on search depth or compute budget,

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:14.400
the system can drift toward ever more complex but increasingly fragile configurations

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.720
that overfit to the validation set.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.720
So when should you actually reach for this approach?

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:24.760
I'd say use it for any recurring domain task where you can define clear success metrics

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:28.840
and you're willing to spend 10 to 100 times more upfront compute

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:31.040
to get a truly specialized harness.

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:36.360
The old alternative of hand crafting prompts across many different models

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:39.880
scales extremely poorly as task complexity grows.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:44.420
Moving from frontier agent techniques to practical local deployment,

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:48.760
this week also saw strong community validation of the new Gemma 4 models

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:53.840
as genuinely useful local agents, especially the 26 billion parameter mixture

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:56.500
of experts variant when running on constrained hardware.

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:01.680
The local LLM community ran a fascinating 30-question blind test covering

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:05.240
code, reasoning, analysis, communication, and meta alignment.

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:08.820
With Claude Opus, 4.6 acting as the judge.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:11.400
The results were closer than many expected,

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:16.420
twin 3.527b, one 46.7% of the matchups,

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.580
but suffered noticeable format and refusal failures on about 10% of questions.

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:26.400
Meanwhile, both Gemma 4.31b and the 26bA for BMOE variant

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.740
achieved identical average scores of 8.82, remarkably competitive.

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:35.720
Gemma 4.31b particularly dominated in communication and writing tasks,

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:40.160
while the 26b mixture of experts version showed impressive efficiency

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:41.400
when it didn't error out.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:45.920
Even more impressively, Gemma 4.31b placed third on the challenging food truck

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:51.480
bench beating GLM5, twin 3.5397b and several Claude Sonnet variants.

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:56.120
On the practical side, community reports are increasingly highlighting Gemma 4,

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:59.120
especially that 26bA for BMOE.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:03.980
As an exceptionally strong local agent for Windows machines and 16 gigabyte hardware.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:10.900
Users consistently reported outperforming twin 3.527b in real-world coding speed,

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:15.280
multilingual support, systems and DevOps tasks, and creative work.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.360
People are getting impressive results with far fewer prompts and much less looping behavior,

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:24.060
things like generating clean SVGs on the first or second try,

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:28.880
or building a surprisingly capable Doom-style raycaster in HTML and JavaScript.

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:33.720
Alibaba's twin team also dropped an interesting new reasoning algorithm this week

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:36.820
that could have significant downstream impact on future models.

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:41.620
They introduced a reinforcement learning variant that waits each reasoning step

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:44.240
by its actual influence on future tokens,

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:47.980
rather than applying the traditional uniform reward across all steps.

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:53.220
Early reports suggest this change can roughly double the length of coherent thought processes

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:55.840
in reasoning models before they start to degrade.

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:00.640
This feels like a meaningful step toward more reliable long-chain reasoning,

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:05.480
especially for local models that need to maximize capability within tight compute budgets.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:07.580
On the agent and tooling front,

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:12.180
there were several notable releases that make local and autonomous workflows much more practical.

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:20.280
First, there's now a one-click open-class setup that combines Gemma 4 and twin 3.5 with turboquant caching and context warming.

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:25.460
This makes viable local agents possible on a 16-gigabyte MacBook Air or Mac Mini

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:27.420
at 10-15 tokens per second.

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:32.140
The team also pushed patches to the law-mod.cpp turboquant implementation

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:34.440
that significantly improved tool-calling reliability.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:37.240
In the practical tools section,

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:42.100
the Kreitsberg document intelligence library just hit version 4.7.0.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:47.340
This rust core extraction library now supports 248 languages through tree-sitter

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:52.620
and delivers AST-level code intelligence for functions, classes, imports, and symbols.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:54.540
The improvements are striking.

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:58.220
Structural F1 scores jumped across 23 different formats.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:01.620
LiteX went from 0% to 100%,

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:07.980
and PDF table extraction improved from 15.5% to 53.7%.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:12.720
It now ships as a clean open-web UI backend and includes a new tune format

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:16.600
that reduces token usage in agent prompts by 30-50%.

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:22.320
This is going to be a game-changer for any agent that needs to deeply understand code bases or complex documents.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:29.520
Simon Willison also released a small but very useful Python tool called Scan for Secrets, version 0.1.

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:35.920
It scans directories for API keys and secrets, including common encodings and escaping variants.

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:40.900
Built with Cloud Code using read-me-driven development and red-green test-driven development,

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:46.600
it's perfect for safely publishing transcripts of local agent sessions without accidentally leaking credentials.

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:52.780
Finally, there's a new project called Cadenza that adds intelligent 1B integration for agents.

00:08:53.220 --> 00:09:00.440
The CLI and Python SDK can import 1B projects, index runs by configuration and metrics,

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:04.200
and return only the highest performing experiments to your agents.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:08.940
It's specifically designed to reduce context rot and help autonomous research loops

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:11.860
make better exploration versus exploitation trade-offs.

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:14.320
Here are the things you should try this week.

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:19.820
1. Take auto agent and point the meta-optimizer at one of your existing agent tasks.

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:23.540
Give it your current harness for terminal bench style coding or data analysis

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:27.620
and see how many iterations it takes to outperform your manually tuned version.

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:29.420
The results can be eye-opening.

