Try it on coding and vision tasks on 16GB hardware.

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:50.420
Use temperature 0.3, Min-P0.1, and Image-Min-Token's 300 for strong vision performance.

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:56.800
3. Test Kreutzberg 4.7.0 as your new document extraction backend.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:03.880
Feed it code repositories or mixed PDFs and spreadsheets, then compare token efficiency using the new tune format.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:10.240
4. If you're running autonomous research agents into great cadenza with your WAN B projects,

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:13.020
the reduction in context rot is dramatic.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:20.760
5. Get in the habit of running scan for secrets before publishing any local clawed code or agent transcripts,

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:24.200
especially detailed logs from auto agent or open claw runs.

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:29.640
On the horizon, we should expect more releases and quant optimizations for the Gemma 4 family

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:33.920
as the community continues pushing local agent performance on consumer hardware.

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:41.200
The twin teams per step weighted reinforcement learning technique will likely make its way into future twin 3.6 releases.

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:47.860
We'll also probably see more agent frameworks adopting structured document intelligence backends like Kreutzberg

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:53.800
now that it offers clean AST level code extraction and smooth open web UI integration.

00:10:53.800 --> 00:11:01.060
And I expect the auto agent pattern of same model critic loops to spread rapidly through other open source agent projects.

